WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.978
Local models should be evaluated against real workflow needs.

2
00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:13.660
A model that sounds fluent may still miss instructions, invent details, ignore formatting, overstate certainty, or fail on domain-specific language.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.788
Evaluation turns model selection from guesswork into evidence.

4
00:00:17.788 --> 00:00:21.315
Repeatable prompt sets are a practical starting point.

5
00:00:21.315 --> 00:00:31.523
Create representative prompts for the work the model will actually support: drafting, summarization, extraction, coding assistance, comparison, or review.

6
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:35.801
Run the same prompts across candidate models and note what changes.

7
00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:39.553
Quality is more than whether the output is pleasant to read.

8
00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:51.787
Track hallucinations, missing constraints, formatting problems, unsafe assumptions, refusal behavior where appropriate, latency, stability, and how often a human must repair the result.

9
00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:56.290
These observations help the team choose a model that fits the workflow.

10
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.142
Acceptance criteria should match task risk.

11
00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:10.625
A brainstorming assistant can tolerate more uncertainty than a workflow that influences customer communication, compliance review, security review, or operational decisions.

12
00:01:10.625 --> 00:01:15.579
Higher-risk workflows need stronger human review and clearer quality gates.

13
00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:25.186
Document the findings. Record which model was tested, what prompts were used, what settings mattered, what failed, and what the model is approved to do.

14
00:01:25.186 --> 00:01:31.715
Good evaluation makes local AI workflows easier to defend, improve, and retire when they no longer fit.

15
00:01:31.715 --> 00:01:36.519
It also helps users avoid repeating the same model comparison from scratch.

16
00:01:36.519 --> 00:01:42.898
Evaluation notes should be plain enough that another maintainer can understand the decision later.
